
INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, content, meta_description, meta_keywords, cover_image_url, author, is_published, published_at)
VALUES
(
  'Meet Pix Chat: Your Free AI Assistant for Everyday Tasks',
  'meet-pix-chat-ai-assistant',
  'Pix Chat is a free, advanced AI assistant built into Pix Engineer. Explore ideas, solve problems, write code, translate, and learn faster — no signup required.',
  E'# Meet Pix Chat: Your Free AI Assistant for Everyday Tasks\n\nPix Chat is the newest addition to Pix Engineer — a fast, friendly, and genuinely useful AI assistant that helps you think, write, code, translate, and learn. It works right in your browser, on desktop or mobile, and you can start chatting instantly with **no signup required**.\n\n![Pix Chat welcome screen](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1531746790731-6c087fecd65a?w=1200&q=80)\n\n## Why we built Pix Chat\n\nThe rest of Pix Engineer focuses on shipping software — App Builder, Design to Code, and Prompt Engineer help you go from an idea to a live product. But we kept hearing the same feedback: *"I love the app tools, but I also want a general-purpose AI for the rest of my day."*\n\nPix Chat is that assistant. Same account, same credits, same clean UI — but pointed at everyday thinking.\n\n## What you can do with Pix Chat\n\n### 1. Explore ideas\nAsk open-ended questions and let the model help you brainstorm. It is genuinely useful for:\n- Naming a product or a domain\n- Outlining a blog post or a talk\n- Comparing two frameworks or two career paths\n- Sanity-checking an argument before you send that Slack message\n\n### 2. Write code\nPix Chat is powered by the same premium models we use in App Builder. Paste a stack trace, ask for a regex, request a small utility function — you get a working answer with an explanation.\n\n```ts\nfunction debounce<T extends (...args: unknown[]) => void>(fn: T, ms = 250) {\n  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;\n  return (...args: Parameters<T>) => {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Learn faster\nAsk for an ELI5, then ask for a college-level version, then ask for the mathematical proof. Because Pix Chat keeps context in the thread, you can drill into a topic without repeating yourself.\n\n### 4. Translate and rewrite\nDrop in a paragraph in any language and ask for a natural translation, a formal rewrite, a friendlier tone, or a tighter version. Great for emails, LinkedIn posts, or documentation.\n\n### 5. Search the web (optional)\nToggle **Web search** on a message and Pix Chat will pull in fresh results before answering. Useful for anything time-sensitive: pricing, releases, news, or docs.\n\n![Person working with an AI assistant](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1553877522-43269d4ea984?w=1200&q=80)\n\n## Free plan, real limits, no surprises\n\nWe want Pix Chat to be genuinely usable without a credit card, so:\n\n- **Anonymous users** get instant access to auto-routed free models. No signup, no rate-limit games.\n- **Signed-in users on the free plan** get the same free models plus **saved chat history** across devices.\n- **Paid credits** unlock our top-tier models (currently GPT-5.3 / Gemini 3 class) with faster, sharper answers.\n\nIf your paid credits run out, we automatically fall back to free models and keep the conversation going. No dead ends.\n\n## Privacy and history\n\nWhen you are signed in, threads are stored in your Pix Engineer account and shown in the sidebar. You can rename, delete, or start a fresh chat at any time. When you are signed out, nothing is persisted — the conversation lives only in your current tab.\n\n## Tips to get better answers\n\n1. **Give context up front.** "I''m writing a landing page for a B2B analytics tool" beats a cold "write a headline".\n2. **Ask for the format.** Say "bullet list", "table", "JSON", "step-by-step" — the model respects it.\n3. **Iterate.** If the first answer is 80%, reply with "shorter and more concrete" or "keep the structure, change the tone to playful".\n4. **Use web search for anything after your model''s training cutoff.**\n5. **Upload an image** when you want the model to look at a screenshot, chart, or design.\n\n## Where Pix Chat fits in Pix Engineer\n\n- **App Builder** — build full-stack apps by chatting with an AI that writes React, Tailwind, and Supabase code.\n- **Design to Code** — turn a Figma export or a screenshot into production HTML/CSS/React.\n- **Prompt Engineer** — craft, refine, and analyze prompts for any AI model.\n- **Pix Chat** — everything else: ideas, code snippets, writing, learning, translation.\n\nAll four share one account, one credit balance, and one design system.\n\n## Try it now\n\nOpen [Pix Chat](/chat) and ask it something you were about to Google. If you like it, [sign in](/signup) to save your history and unlock the premium models.\n\nWe read every piece of feedback — [tell us what to build next](/contact).',
  'Pix Chat is a free, advanced AI assistant inside Pix Engineer. Chat with premium AI models to brainstorm, code, translate, and learn — no signup required.',
  'Pix Chat, AI assistant, ChatGPT alternative, free AI chat, AI chatbot, Pix Engineer, GPT-5, Gemini 3',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1531746790731-6c087fecd65a?w=800&q=80',
  'Pix Engineer Team',
  true,
  now()
),
(
  '10 Ways to Use Pix Chat to Get More Done Every Day',
  'pix-chat-productivity-guide',
  'A practical playbook for using Pix Chat as your daily AI copilot — from inbox triage and meeting prep to code reviews, learning plans, and creative brainstorming.',
  E'# 10 Ways to Use Pix Chat to Get More Done Every Day\n\nMost people who try an AI assistant use it for two or three things, then forget about it. That is a shame, because a good chat model is closer to a **thinking partner** than a search box. Here are ten concrete workflows we use every day with [Pix Chat](/chat) — steal any that fit.\n\n![Focused workspace with laptop and notebook](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517430816045-df4b7de11d1d?w=1200&q=80)\n\n## 1. Inbox triage in the morning\n\nPaste the subject lines (or full bodies) of the emails waiting in your inbox and ask:\n\n> "Group these into: needs a reply today, can wait a week, and safe to archive. For the urgent ones, draft a two-sentence reply."\n\nYou will process an hour of email in ten minutes.\n\n## 2. Meeting prep in 60 seconds\n\nBefore a call, paste the invite, the person''s LinkedIn bio, and last week''s notes. Ask:\n\n> "Give me three things to open with, two risks to raise, and one specific ask."\n\nYou will walk in with a plan.\n\n## 3. Turn a rough thought into a written doc\n\nStart with a voice-memo-style brain dump — no structure, just what you think. Then ask Pix Chat to turn it into a one-page brief, a PRD, or a Loom script. Edit the output; do not accept it blindly.\n\n## 4. Code review a diff before you push\n\nPaste your git diff and ask:\n\n> "Act as a senior reviewer. Flag anything that would fail a code review at a well-run startup: bugs, security issues, dead code, weak names, missing tests. Be blunt."\n\nCatches more than you think.\n\n![Developer at a code review](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1555099962-4199c345e5dd?w=1200&q=80)\n\n## 5. Build a personal learning plan\n\nSay you want to learn Rust, or Postgres internals, or negotiation. Ask:\n\n> "Design a 4-week learning plan for [topic]. Assume 30 minutes a day. Include a project I ship at the end of each week."\n\nThen do week 1. Come back next Monday and refine.\n\n## 6. Debug with the model as rubber duck\n\nPaste the error, the relevant code, and what you have already tried. Say:\n\n> "Do not give me the answer yet. Ask me questions that would help you diagnose this."\n\nThe conversation itself often finds the bug.\n\n## 7. Rewrite for a specific audience\n\nWrite the message the way you would say it to a peer. Then ask:\n\n> "Rewrite this for my CEO. Keep it under 120 words, lead with the ask, and remove hedging."\n\nGreat for status updates, escalations, and skip-level pings.\n\n## 8. Translate anything, in tone\n\nPix Chat does not just translate the words — you can ask for **formal Japanese for a customer** or **friendly Brazilian Portuguese for a Slack DM**. Enable **Web search** if you need current slang or product names.\n\n## 9. Compare options without bias\n\nDeciding between two tools, two job offers, or two architectures? Ask:\n\n> "Give me a table: dimensions on the left, both options on the right, honest trade-offs in each cell. Then tell me which one you would pick and why."\n\nThe forced structure reveals what you were avoiding.\n\n## 10. Creative sprints\n\nStuck on a name, a headline, an illustration prompt, a color palette? Ask for **twenty options**, then pick your three favorites and ask for twenty more in that direction. You will land somewhere unexpected — in a good way.\n\n![Creative brainstorm session](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?w=1200&q=80)\n\n## Getting the most out of Pix Chat\n\n- **Keep one thread per topic.** Pix Chat remembers everything in the current conversation. Start a new thread when you switch projects so context stays clean.\n- **Turn on Web search** for anything current: docs, pricing, news, launches.\n- **Attach images** — screenshots of bugs, whiteboard photos, chart PNGs. The model can read them.\n- **Sign in** to save your history across devices. Anonymous chats vanish when the tab closes.\n- **Upgrade** when you find yourself hitting the free tier daily. Paid credits unlock GPT-5.3 / Gemini 3 class models and much faster answers.\n\n## Where to start\n\nPick one of the ten workflows above and try it right now:\n\n[Open Pix Chat →](/chat)\n\nAnd if you want an AI that goes beyond chat — one that actually **writes and ships full-stack apps** — try [App Builder](/) or [Design to Code](/design-to-code). Pix Chat is your thinking partner; App Builder is your engineering team.',
  'Ten practical ways to use Pix Chat every day — inbox triage, meeting prep, code review, learning plans, translation, and creative brainstorming.',
